### 3.2 Stratum B — No Containers and language as structure

The original `no_containers.md` is missing, so its exact text is known only through the Claude-side capture and the axiom deck. The recurring three-part ladder is nevertheless clear:

1. Words do not carry payloads.
2. Sensorimotor grounding does not secretly reintroduce a true payload; it adds more occurrence structure.
3. Relationships form objects; objects are not primitive things later decorated with relationships.

The “blue sky” substitution test expresses relabeling invariance: if every occurrence and relation is relabeled consistently, the structure remains the same. Communication therefore cannot be the shipment of contents. Each side reconstructs locally from its own history; understanding is overlap sufficient for useful coordination.

This is the reason SHOE LACEY rejects an external semantic authority. It is also why “meaning” at HCC scale must be an operation over history rather than contents stored inside a word, star, or object.

